      "id": 1,
      "text": "Goldville was founded in 1842 during Alabama's peak gold‑mining period, named for the gold found nearby.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/goldville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "At its height, Goldville's temporary post office handled more mail in a day than New York City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldville%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Goldville incorporated on January 25, 1843 and became one of Alabama's largest towns at the time.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/goldville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town once boasted a race track, saloons, a hotel, and a masonic lodge, reflecting its bustling gold‑rush economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/goldville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Goldville reincorporated in July 1973 after nearly a century of dormancy, reestablishing its municipal government.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/goldville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Goldville is one of only four Alabama towns with circular town limits, a unique geographic quirk.",
